course: technology
lesson: 12
title: Washing, drying, vacuuming and ironing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Cleaning machines do the boring jobs people once did by hand: scrubbing clothes, wringing them out, beating carpets and pressing creases.
explain: Most of them use one of a few tricks: spinning very fast, blowing air, sucking air in, or heating with an element.
explain: Watch a washing machine on spin: the drum whirls so fast that the water is flung out through the little holes, just like water flying off a dog that shakes itself.
explain: A vacuum cleaner does not really suck at all: a fan pushes air out, and the air outside pushes in, carrying the crumbs with it.
explain: These machines are strong and some get hot, so they come with rules: no climbing in, no fingers near, and no water near anything plugged in.
fact: A washing machine fills with water, turns the clothes back and forth so they rub with soap, drains, rinses, and then spins fast so the water flies out through the holes in the drum.
fact: A washing machine spin can turn the drum more than a thousand times a minute, and the door stays locked while it works.
fact: A tumble dryer blows warm air through tumbling clothes to carry the water away, and its filter catches fluff, which is tiny bits of your clothes and must be cleaned every time because it can catch fire.
fact: A dishwasher's spinning arms squirt hot soapy water at the dishes; the water pushing out of the arms makes them spin, and it often uses less water than washing up by hand; sharp knives go in the basket point down.
fact: A vacuum cleaner's fan pushes air out, leaving lower pressure inside (less air pushing), so the air outside rushes in through the hose carrying dust.
fact: A bagless vacuum spins the air like a whirlwind, flinging dust to the sides of the bin where it drops; the idea came from big dust separators in sawmills.
fact: An iron's element heats the base plate and a thermostat holds it at the setting; steam helps the cloth fibres relax and lie flat.
fact: A hairdryer blows air over hot wires, and has a cut-out that switches it off if it overheats.
fact: Clothes on a drying rack dry because the water slowly evaporates into the air; wind and warmth make it faster, and clothes dry even in cold weather, often fastest on a windy day.
fact: Detergent pods hold strong soap in a film that dissolves in water; they look like sweets but are poisonous, so they are kept locked away from little children.
fact: If clothes bunch on one side, the spinning drum wobbles and the machine can shake or even walk across the floor; heavy concrete blocks inside many machines help keep it steady.
fact: Never climb into a washing machine or dryer, never use a hairdryer near a bath or sink, because water and electricity together can kill, never vacuum pets, hair or fingers, and never touch a hot iron or leave it face down.
exercise: How does spinning get water out of washing? | The water is flung out through the holes in the drum | The spin heats the water up until it all disappears as steam | The drum squeezes the clothes flat | A pump sucks the water out of each sock
exercise: Why does the washing machine door lock while it works? | So nobody opens it full of water or while it spins | So the clothes cannot escape | So the soap stays fresh | So the machine knows it is daytime
exercise: Where does the fluff in a tumble dryer filter come from? | Tiny bits rubbed off your clothes | Dust sucked in from the room | Soap that dried into fluff | Hair from the people in the house
exercise: Why must the dryer's fluff filter be cleaned every time? | Fluff can catch fire | Fluff makes the clothes wet again | Fluff makes the drum spin backwards | Fluff turns into mould at once
exercise: What makes the arms in a dishwasher spin? | The water pushing out of them | A tiny motor hidden inside each arm | The heat of the hot water inside it | The weight of the dishes pressing down
exercise: Which way do sharp knives go in a dishwasher basket? | Point down | Point up | Lying on the top rack | It doesn't matter at all
exercise: How does a vacuum cleaner pick up dust? | A fan pushes air out, and outside air pushes in carrying dust | A magnet in the hose pulls the dust in | The brush throws dust into the bag | Static electricity on the hose makes the dust jump up and into it
exercise: Why does the dust spin round in the bin of a bagless vacuum? | The air whirls like a whirlwind and flings it to the sides | A little paddle stirs it round | The dust is magnetic | The bin turns on a turntable
exercise: How does steam help get rid of creases? | It helps the cloth fibres relax and lie flat | It glues the fibres of the cloth flat with water | It washes the creases out of the cloth like soap | It shrinks the cloth so tight that creases vanish
exercise: What does a hairdryer do? | Blows air over hot wires and out of the nozzle | Sucks the water out of your hair through tiny holes | Heats your hair with microwaves | Blows cold air that turns warm on its own
exercise: Why do clothes dry even on a cold day? | Water still slowly evaporates into the air | The cold air squeezes the water out of them | They only look dry, but really they stay wet inside | Frost forms on them and soaks up all the water
exercise: Why do detergent pods disappear in the wash? | Their film dissolves in water | They are eaten by the drum | They melt only in hot sunshine | They get washed down the drain whole
exercise: Why are detergent pods kept locked away from toddlers? | They look like sweets but are poisonous | They explode if dropped | They make toys go soapy | They are very expensive
exercise: Why does a washing machine shake and walk across the floor? | Clothes bunched on one side make the drum unbalanced | It has too little soap in it | The floor is too smooth | The water inside is too cold
exercise: Why is a washing machine so heavy? | Many have a concrete block inside to keep them steady | They are made of solid iron | They are always full of water | The motor weighs as much as a car
exercise: Mum puts 3 pairs of socks and 2 odd socks in the wash. How many socks go in? | 8 | 5 | 6 | 7
test: my mate says a hoover sucks the dirt in like a straw sucks juice. so what's really pushing? | The air outside pushes in where the pressure is lower | The hoover's magnet pulls the dirt | The dirt jumps in by itself | The carpet pushes the dirt up
test: shut up, I'm hiding in the washing machine for hide and seek. What's the rule? | Never climb in a washing machine or dryer | It's fine as long as the door is left open a bit | It's fine if the machine is unplugged | It's fine if you are small enough
test: Jake dries his hair in the bath with the hairdryer plugged in. What should happen? | Stop: water and electricity together can kill | It's fine as long as he keeps his feet up out of the water | It's fine with a cordless phone nearby | It's fine if the water is cold
test: Which day dries washing on the line fastest, most often? | A windy day | A still, foggy day | A still, damp day | A rainy day
test: What would happen if the clothes all bunched up on one side during the spin? | The drum would wobble and the machine might shake | The clothes would come out of the drum much drier than usual | The spin would go twice as fast | The machine would fill with soap
test: The iron's still on the board, mum's gone to get the phone. can I finish my t-shirt? | No, leave the hot iron to an adult | Yes, as long as you are really quick | Yes, irons cool down within a minute | No, but you can carry it away to the sink
test: In a cartoon a vacuum sucks up a whole puppy. What's the real rule? | Never vacuum pets, hair or fingers | Only big vacuums can suck up a puppy | It's fine on the lowest setting, gently | Puppies like being vacuumed, it tickles
test: My brother says the dishwasher always wastes more water than washing by hand. Right? | No, a dishwasher often uses less | Yes, it uses a full bath each time | Yes, three times as much | No, it uses no water at all
test: Why does a hairdryer have a cut-out switch? | To switch off if it gets too hot | To make it louder | To change the colour of the air | To save the battery
test: A little one finds a pod that looks like a sweet. What do you do? | Take it away and tell an adult at once | Let them lick it once | Put it back in the open box | Wash it under the tap and give it back
test: Mia says the fluff in the dryer is dust from outside. What is it really? | Tiny bits of the clothes being dried | Dust blown in from the street outside the house | Mould growing in the machine | Soap that dried out
exam: Identify the principle by which a domestic vacuum cleaner collects dust. | Air at greater external pressure flows into a region of reduced pressure | A magnetic field attracts particles of dust | Rotating brushes propel dust into a sealed compartment | Static charge causes dust to adhere to the hose
exam: It is given that the lint filter of a tumble dryer has not been emptied. Identify the principal hazard thereby created. | The accumulated fluff may ignite | The garments may be rendered damp | The drum may rotate in reverse | The machine may consume no electricity
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of a washing machine moving across the floor during its spin cycle? | An uneven distribution of garments unbalances the drum | An insufficient quantity of detergent was used in the wash | The water supplied was excessively cold | The machine was installed upon a carpet
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning detergent pods. | They resemble confectionery yet are poisonous if swallowed | They are harmless if swallowed by an infant, being made of soap | They dissolve only in direct sunlight | They contain no soap whatsoever
